"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxzcOCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAvSEQAERVZFsr/lfwQ/geKg0GXFHk\nuKU4n2CUC2Lz0TJR5mlj9Tbg2Vc5vxkeN8OJznpdLg6ZeAKqpZksJ9OnxchZnKp8\nSmL/xxQAFxPWFjL8v3PznMIuAUxK1R/Tx0lv4Eq+ImArYeY7v8lAK2NmRQ3KxdJo\n9Y80J7eoj4iKRW/yDMG6uVX8eeC34L9lkzd4ccCFJMOCiXxhPhjVjmiy7YAiIxHR\nOw3iOOZ5ZeUhnAMN8Xk+Q86CCp+HbczCQp9YdPqLNLxP/94IgVgoH9nLSfX+/7uW\nnfYRFX97ORdZtxar4GllexH/gJAelP3VwRPakr1bupkYTy0nJ94PN/etJFU5mSoo\nlWqIi6Rqo4F//LNA8VzAw2MVgWlY8N3tVp+BdZbAIr3JRJe1aYFde6mXUGFT2Z8O\ntN+Lsa+2o/A777qQ3cF+iELrhqbHzG47z5ah6V2AGJzo4g7kFjTdUvwfcHxYkDbR\nfUspkLdFrnOfsUaWv6uxunJlYBIKJG+q91VsU4yKVBrB+glXJcUA5aLiQ5SMGIM1\nsXFVIfXbEJbRI3JZysaekPwm/DhKNd15vYJh0t8EO8bvjuvTPuvjXaQ8fxkj9GDw\nwm1Cm5ZuV6y/0AdrlyJN6n1fU1kbD4nxrkNMjNr3sESCcReM6XOvPIDro5Bc1Pxm\n4q9Rmg6z5L/785obcFfb\n=Xa4q\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-08T06:24:14Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/fallow-rs/fallow/commit/ca633ea2b1c668ea36f1e3963b340e8ddc16275c","node_id":"C_kwDORpPJVNoAKGNhNjMzZWEyYjFjNjY4ZWEzNmYxZTM5NjNiMzQwZThkZGMxNjI3NWM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/fallow-rs/fallow/commit/7dfb1f9756449bab3c7f3654dcbd0b78553fe8c1","sha":"7dfb1f9756449bab3c7f3654dcbd0b78553fe8c1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/7dfb1f9756449bab3c7f3654dcbd0b78553fe8c1"}],"sha":"ca633ea2b1c668ea36f1e3963b340e8ddc16275c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/ca633ea2b1c668ea36f1e3963b340e8ddc16275c"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1352352?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/BartWaardenburg","id":1352352,"login":"BartWaardenburg","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNTIzNTI=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/7dfb1f9756449bab3c7f3654dcbd0b78553fe8c1/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-07T20:46:28Z","email":"bart@waardenburg.dev","name":"Bart Waardenburg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-07T20:46:28Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(cli): add fallow architecture command (#3275)\n\n* feat(cli): add fallow architecture command\n\nAdd `fallow architecture` for import cycles, boundary violations and rule-pack policy violations. The command runs the dead-code analysis with these issue types selected, so the JSON envelope, finding ids, exit codes and baselines are the same as for the dead-code structure flags.\n\n- `--cycles`, `--boundaries` and `--policy` select one kind.\n- The dead-code structure flags stay as deprecated aliases.\n- Plain `fallow dead-code` keeps the findings and prints one stderr hint in human output.\n- Bare `fallow` human output shows an Architecture section.\n- The help text and the task matrix put guard and architecture together.\n- The dead-code summary lists policy violations, which the total already counted.\n- Drift invariant I14 compares the command with the dead-code structure flags.\n\n* docs: document fallow architecture and deprecate dead-code structure flags\n\nList the new command in the README, the CHANGELOG and the compatibility notes. Record the five deprecated dead-code flags and the default report of structure findings by dead-code until the next major version. Add drift invariant I14 to the drift contract.\n\n* fix(cli): drop the unused --top flag from fallow architecture\n\nThe architecture sections never read --top, so the flag had no effect.\nThe skill docs no longer use a deprecated flag as a dead-code example,\nand the cli reference names the --group-by heading limit.\n\n* feat(cli): record fallow architecture as its own telemetry workflow\n\nTelemetry recorded `fallow architecture` runs as the `dead_code` workflow. The runs now use the `architecture` workflow. The workflow surfaces findings, so every exit path notes findings_present, as the shared dead-code exit paths already do. A follow-up run of the workflow uses the `check` follow-up kind.\n\n* feat(cli): accept architecture in bare --only and --skip\n\nBare `fallow` now accepts `architecture` in `--only` and `--skip`. The\narchitecture findings come from the dead-code analysis:\n\n- `--only architecture` runs that analysis and reports only import\n  cycles, boundary violations and policy violations.\n- `--skip architecture` removes these findings from the dead-code\n  section.\n- With `dead-code` in the same list, the value has no extra effect.\n\nThe selection applies after the baseline stage. The `--save-baseline`\nfile and the health input stay the same as in a run without the value.\nThe regression counts follow the narrowed section, and a narrowed run\ndoes not write the whole-project impact track. Output of runs without\nthe value does not change.\n\nBare `fallow --group-by` and `--summary` now show the architecture\nfindings under one \"Architecture\" label, like the flat layout.\n`fallow architecture --group-by` uses one \"Architecture\" heading per\ngroup instead of \"Structure\" and \"Policy\".\n\n* feat(ci): accept the architecture command in the Action and GitLab template\n\nThe GitHub Action now accepts `command: architecture` and the GitLab\ntemplate accepts `FALLOW_COMMAND: architecture`. The command writes the\ndead-code envelope, so both integrations use the dead-code paths for the\nissue count, the SARIF file, the summary and the annotations.\n\n- The `issue-types` input and `FALLOW_ISSUE_TYPES` take the architecture\n  filters: cycles, boundaries and policy.\n- The regression inputs and the baseline inputs apply to the command.\n- The count gate and the advisory line say \"architecture issues\".\n- The invalid-command error lists the command.\n\nA real `fallow architecture` envelope is the test fixture for both suites.\n\nRefs #3271\n\n* docs(changelog): note the architecture command in the Action and GitLab template\n\n* fix(cli): name the architecture category in quiet --only architecture runs\n\nQuiet bare runs with --only architecture printed the findings under\nStructure and a Dead Code Summary heading. The scoped regression warning\nnow also names a path and the architecture selection. The --boundaries\nhelp text names boundary coverage and forbidden calls, the Action and\nGitLab selector docs list architecture, and the cli reference states the\nstale-baseline behavior. A telemetry test pins findings_present=true for\nan architecture run with a cycle.\n\n* fix(ci): reject unknown architecture issue types in the Action and GitLab template\n\nThe architecture command accepts only cycles, boundaries and policy as\nissue-types values. Another value now stops the job with exit code 2 and\nan error that names the valid values. The include-entry-exports\ndescription says that the input does not apply to architecture, and the\nbaseline-saved-by description says that an architecture baseline reports\ndead-code.\n\n* fix(cli): show architecture findings as a Dead Code category in bare fallow\n\nBare fallow printed the Architecture heading on stderr at the level of\nthe section headings. The dead-code status line then printed under\nArchitecture, and with stdout in a file the findings showed under\nMaintenance. The findings now print as an Architecture category on\nstdout after the other dead-code categories, with and without --quiet.\n\nOther changes for the architecture command:\n\n- The status line counts policy, boundary coverage and boundary call\n  violations, and names boundary violations as such.\n- The Failed line of --only architecture names architecture. It uses\n  the singular for one issue and one clone group.\n- --summary prints no blank line above Architecture when no dead-code\n  row precedes it.\n- The command help has one description everywhere.\n- JSON next_steps and the --explain docs URL name architecture for an\n  architecture run. Dead-code JSON does not change.\n- The findings_present telemetry purpose lists the architecture and\n  security workflows, as docs/telemetry.md does.\n\n* docs(changelog): merge the two Fixed subsections after the rebase","tree":{"sha":"7890f35931baf202a2d08321270a7a1e587c5d5e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/git/trees/7890f35931baf202a2d08321270a7a1e587c5d5e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/git/commits/7dfb1f9756449bab3c7f3654dcbd0b78553fe8c1","verification":{"payload":"tree 7890f35931baf202a2d08321270a7a1e587c5d5e\nparent 0e43f2c68c6c295d9a4091d5dc1efdc40a3335b7\nauthor Bart Waardenburg <bart@waardenburg.dev> 1791405988 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791405988 +0200\n\nfeat(cli): add fallow architecture command (#3275)\n\n* feat(cli): add fallow architecture command\n\nAdd `fallow architecture` for import cycles, boundary violations and rule-pack policy violations. The command runs the dead-code analysis with these issue types selected, so the JSON envelope, finding ids, exit codes and baselines are the same as for the dead-code structure flags.\n\n- `--cycles`, `--boundaries` and `--policy` select one kind.\n- The dead-code structure flags stay as deprecated aliases.\n- Plain `fallow dead-code` keeps the findings and prints one stderr hint in human output.\n- Bare `fallow` human output shows an Architecture section.\n- The help text and the task matrix put guard and architecture together.\n- The dead-code summary lists policy violations, which the total already counted.\n- Drift invariant I14 compares the command with the dead-code structure flags.\n\n* docs: document fallow architecture and deprecate dead-code structure flags\n\nList the new command in the README, the CHANGELOG and the compatibility notes. Record the five deprecated dead-code flags and the default report of structure findings by dead-code until the next major version. Add drift invariant I14 to the drift contract.\n\n* fix(cli): drop the unused --top flag from fallow architecture\n\nThe architecture sections never read --top, so the flag had no effect.\nThe skill docs no longer use a deprecated flag as a dead-code example,\nand the cli reference names the --group-by heading limit.\n\n* feat(cli): record fallow architecture as its own telemetry workflow\n\nTelemetry recorded `fallow architecture` runs as the `dead_code` workflow. The runs now use the `architecture` workflow. The workflow surfaces findings, so every exit path notes findings_present, as the shared dead-code exit paths already do. A follow-up run of the workflow uses the `check` follow-up kind.\n\n* feat(cli): accept architecture in bare --only and --skip\n\nBare `fallow` now accepts `architecture` in `--only` and `--skip`. The\narchitecture findings come from the dead-code analysis:\n\n- `--only architecture` runs that analysis and reports only import\n  cycles, boundary violations and policy violations.\n- `--skip architecture` removes these findings from the dead-code\n  section.\n- With `dead-code` in the same list, the value has no extra effect.\n\nThe selection applies after the baseline stage. The `--save-baseline`\nfile and the health input stay the same as in a run without the value.\nThe regression counts follow the narrowed section, and a narrowed run\ndoes not write the whole-project impact track. Output of runs without\nthe value does not change.\n\nBare `fallow --group-by` and `--summary` now show the architecture\nfindings under one \"Architecture\" label, like the flat layout.\n`fallow architecture --group-by` uses one \"Architecture\" heading per\ngroup instead of \"Structure\" and \"Policy\".\n\n* feat(ci): accept the architecture command in the Action and GitLab template\n\nThe GitHub Action now accepts `command: architecture` and the GitLab\ntemplate accepts `FALLOW_COMMAND: architecture`. The command writes the\ndead-code envelope, so both integrations use the dead-code paths for the\nissue count, the SARIF file, the summary and the annotations.\n\n- The `issue-types` input and `FALLOW_ISSUE_TYPES` take the architecture\n  filters: cycles, boundaries and policy.\n- The regression inputs and the baseline inputs apply to the command.\n- The count gate and the advisory line say \"architecture issues\".\n- The invalid-command error lists the command.\n\nA real `fallow architecture` envelope is the test fixture for both suites.\n\nRefs #3271\n\n* docs(changelog): note the architecture command in the Action and GitLab template\n\n* fix(cli): name the architecture category in quiet --only architecture runs\n\nQuiet bare runs with --only architecture printed the findings under\nStructure and a Dead Code Summary heading. The scoped regression warning\nnow also names a path and the architecture selection. The --boundaries\nhelp text names boundary coverage and forbidden calls, the Action and\nGitLab selector docs list architecture, and the cli reference states the\nstale-baseline behavior. A telemetry test pins findings_present=true for\nan architecture run with a cycle.\n\n* fix(ci): reject unknown architecture issue types in the Action and GitLab template\n\nThe architecture command accepts only cycles, boundaries and policy as\nissue-types values. Another value now stops the job with exit code 2 and\nan error that names the valid values. The include-entry-exports\ndescription says that the input does not apply to architecture, and the\nbaseline-saved-by description says that an architecture baseline reports\ndead-code.\n\n* fix(cli): show architecture findings as a Dead Code category in bare fallow\n\nBare fallow printed the Architecture heading on stderr at the level of\nthe section headings. The dead-code status line then printed under\nArchitecture, and with stdout in a file the findings showed under\nMaintenance. The findings now print as an Architecture category on\nstdout after the other dead-code categories, with and without --quiet.\n\nOther changes for the architecture command:\n\n- The status line counts policy, boundary coverage and boundary call\n  violations, and names boundary violations as such.\n- The Failed line of --only architecture names architecture. It uses\n  the singular for one issue and one clone group.\n- --summary prints no blank line above Architecture when no dead-code\n  row precedes it.\n- The command help has one description everywhere.\n- JSON next_steps and the --explain docs URL name architecture for an\n  architecture run. Dead-code JSON does not change.\n- The findings_present telemetry purpose lists the architecture and\n  security workflows, as docs/telemetry.md does.\n\n* docs(changelog): merge the two Fixed subsections after the rebase","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxq+kCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAnNQQAIeG2VvvCNrKp8sigRE8B67m\nbnCdLKpPiskaFyLqNto94B4PYAR2Uu/ewoNturegvxzHOkkMECWTOvaJw9tquvL4\nuo5AkCbgbLT2O4F87PtOXIZj7IwNFI/0lmAaT2q08gzzMTU2xtr2NwaZqE2ULfw3\nawyAA3bLbIYvsZwWPPfeC6XRLLVmbRDUL+RJZPger/vQP4DZzKOA4x5twDudV23o\nOVPHntF4b3ORFGwKk3BkNfJvxkqbTG9wo8h69K9iLOrmAmMupXwUpJVmw2aVoqaA\nnufpl+pI+xzPfDUbZwsfYvvKy2Ib4T8OfYJHnH2b6/p7I7Wtt5P4pVunMSI7Vgf5\n/Q2OGtFZsgyJ7i1c44aIMEmlHFreyKkhGQCQ22KOQ3Ud5IOPbCNMvqcjrd45rqeA\n6ktB2fqsx9yQRBawVwZfGWZF95QKlZmBVDLMRIIpfx2WqiWHwa6AY/hxhJSJ96/X\nNpmtZWTvopW1Cdo2j/70FxhPTb9Tvd7+AhFBIUDWLOOZy1M9pY0+RBvEjcQCSkzZ\nxmTepAA//PshLls9LB6K8IuTKkmcbbiDfMspIlwhdckZt9kWaKEREscVWSIK8/So\niyxTKgTujHYoEJWSufKCRNz2lybA9cA+oLlMiCkRmCDX9ic1NNiNNomipcEoILrS\n4vVWKmlQTgyW4BbkOn52\n=Bisb\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-07T20:46:29Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/fallow-rs/fallow/commit/7dfb1f9756449bab3c7f3654dcbd0b78553fe8c1","node_id":"C_kwDORpPJVNoAKDdkZmIxZjk3NTY0NDliYWIzYzdmMzY1NGRjYmQwYjc4NTUzZmU4YzE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/fallow-rs/fallow/commit/0e43f2c68c6c295d9a4091d5dc1efdc40a3335b7","sha":"0e43f2c68c6c295d9a4091d5dc1efdc40a3335b7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/0e43f2c68c6c295d9a4091d5dc1efdc40a3335b7"}],"sha":"7dfb1f9756449bab3c7f3654dcbd0b78553fe8c1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/7dfb1f9756449bab3c7f3654dcbd0b78553fe8c1"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1352352?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/BartWaardenburg","id":1352352,"login":"BartWaardenburg","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNTIzNTI=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/0e43f2c68c6c295d9a4091d5dc1efdc40a3335b7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-07T19:45:57Z","email":"bart@waardenburg.dev","name":"Bart Waardenburg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-07T19:45:57Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test(cli): read the full request in the CLI stub servers (#3274)\n\nThe CLI integration stubs read a request with their own code. The\n`coverage analyze` stub read only once, so it can miss data that arrives\nin a later read. A stub that closes the socket with unread data makes\nWindows abort the connection (os error 10053).\n\nAdd one `read_request` helper in `tests/common/http_stub.rs`. It reads\nthe head, then the body that `content-length` names. The four stubs use\nit, and the three local copies are gone. Unit tests split a request at\neach byte and send a body in a second TCP write.\n\nCloses #3270","tree":{"sha":"70917400a9c44fd7e754b8d9bea8e2c25f629f98","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/git/trees/70917400a9c44fd7e754b8d9bea8e2c25f629f98"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/git/commits/0e43f2c68c6c295d9a4091d5dc1efdc40a3335b7","verification":{"payload":"tree 70917400a9c44fd7e754b8d9bea8e2c25f629f98\nparent 70750281605830e4fb6f3af028f89c6ba25bd990\nauthor Bart Waardenburg <bart@waardenburg.dev> 1791402357 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791402357 +0200\n\ntest(cli): read the full request in the CLI stub servers (#3274)\n\nThe CLI integration stubs read a request with their own code. The\n`coverage analyze` stub read only once, so it can miss data that arrives\nin a later read. A stub that closes the socket with unread data makes\nWindows abort the connection (os error 10053).\n\nAdd one `read_request` helper in `tests/common/http_stub.rs`. It reads\nthe head, then the body that `content-length` names. The four stubs use\nit, and the three local copies are gone. Unit tests split a request at\neach byte and send a body in a second TCP write.\n\nCloses #3270","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxqF1CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA7uEQAEyQUi0SS5XICv+XaUJqaUxK\n2+S50bzDbo3FBSkBZW47BBm2t3DPbE9T8Kuw6iXDolFv