e":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/fallow-rs/fallow/commit/3d03d5e3bcc9796dad15ac0bcaf2af06a5159269","node_id":"C_kwDORpPJVNoAKDNkMDNkNWUzYmNjOTc5NmRhZDE1YWMwYmNhZjJhZjA2YTUxNTkyNjk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/fallow-rs/fallow/commit/253eb87cea6d2401da30c0562c64ab59e587f6e8","sha":"253eb87cea6d2401da30c0562c64ab59e587f6e8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/253eb87cea6d2401da30c0562c64ab59e587f6e8"}],"sha":"3d03d5e3bcc9796dad15ac0bcaf2af06a5159269","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/3d03d5e3bcc9796dad15ac0bcaf2af06a5159269"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1352352?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/BartWaardenburg","id":1352352,"login":"BartWaardenburg","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNTIzNTI=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/253eb87cea6d2401da30c0562c64ab59e587f6e8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T01:19:11Z","email":"bart@waardenburg.dev","name":"Bart Waardenburg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T01:19:11Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(deps): expand optional peers only when the project lists them (#3231)\n\n## Summary\n\nAn optional peer that the project does not list no longer gives credit to its own peers. Before, a listed package could escape the unused-dependency check when only such an unlisted optional peer declared it.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe peer closure in `crates/core/src/analyze/unused_deps.rs` queued every peer it found for further expansion. Since optional peers count as peers, an installed optional peer that the project does not list became a host too. Example:\n\n- The root lists `host` and `leaf` and imports only `host`.\n- `host` declares the optional peer `mid`. `mid` is installed but not listed.\n- `mid` declares `leaf` as a peer (optional or required).\n\nNothing turns on `mid`, so nothing loads `leaf`. But fallow credited `leaf` through `mid`, and `--trace-dependency leaf` said that a used package lists it.\n\n## Fix\n\n- `PeerDependencyResolver` now records for each installed peer whether `peerDependenciesMeta` marks it as optional (`PackageJson::peer_dependency_is_optional`).\n- `peer_dependency_closure` takes the names that the project lists. A required peer is expanded as before. An optional peer is expanded only when the project lists it.\n- The project has one shared install. The root `package.json` or any workspace `package.json` that lists an optional peer turns it on for that install. Thus the root check and every workspace check pass the same set: the root manifest names together with the names of every workspace manifest (`closure_listed_names`).\n- A manifest lists a name when it declares it in any dependency field, peers included. A package that declares the optional peer of a used package as its own peer expects the consumer to install it.\n- The report skips a workspace `package.json` that `ignorePatterns` matches. `peer_dependency_hosts` (used by `--trace-dependency`) now takes the configured ignore patterns, applies the same `package.json` ignore check, and builds its set with the same `closure_listed_names` function. Thus the trace and the report use the same listed set. `fallow_engine::trace::trace_dependency` takes the ignore patterns as a new argument.\n- A listed optional peer of a used package still gets credit, and still passes credit on to its own peers.\n\n## Verification\n\n- New unit test `unlisted_optional_peer_does_not_credit_its_own_peers` and new integration test `an_unlisted_optional_peer_does_not_credit_its_own_peers` with the fixture `tests/fixtures/peer-chain-through-unlisted-peer`. Both failed before the fix (`left: []`, `right: [\"leaf-a\", \"leaf-b\"]`) and pass after it. The integration test also checks the trace output for credited and uncredited leaves.\n- New unit test `optional_peer_listed_by_workspace_credits_root_peer` and new integration test `an_optional_peer_listed_by_a_workspace_credits_a_root_peer` with the fixture `tests/fixtures/peer-chain-through-workspace-peer`. The root lists `leaf`. The workspace lists `host` and its optional peer `mid` and imports `host`. `mid` declares `leaf` as a required peer. Both tests fail when the root closure uses only the root names, and pass with the fix. The integration test also checks that `--trace-dependency leaf` names `mid`.\n- New integration test `an_optional_peer_listed_by_the_root_credits_a_workspace_peer` with the fixture `tests/fixtures/peer-chain-through-root-peer`. The root lists `mid` in `devDependencies`. The workspace `app` lists `host` and `leaf` and imports `host`. `host` declares `mid` as an optional peer, and `mid` declares `leaf` as a required peer. The test failed when the workspace closure used only the workspace names (`app` reported `leaf` as unused) and passes with the fix. It also checks that `--trace-dependency leaf` names `mid`, so the trace and the report agree.\n- New integration test `an_ignored_workspace_does_not_turn_on_an_optional_peer` with the fixture `tests/fixtures/peer-chain-through-ignored-workspace`. The root lists `host` and `leaf` and imports `host`. Only `packages/ignored/package.json` lists the optional peer `mid`, and `ignorePatterns` hides that workspace. The report lists `leaf` as unused. Before this change, `--trace-dependency leaf` said `is_used: true` with `peer_of: [\"mid\"]`. After it, the trace says `is_used: false` with no `peer_of`.\n- New integration test `a_required_peer_credits_its_own_optional_peer` with the fixture `tests/fixtures/peer-chain-through-required-peer`. `host` has the required peer `mid`, and `mid` has the optional peer `leaf`, which the root lists. The report and the trace credit `leaf` through `mid`. This test passed before the change and guards the required-peer path.\n- `cargo fmt --all`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo test -p fallow-core -p fallow-engine -p fallow-api -p fallow-mcp`, the `fallow-cli` integration tests for dependencies and traces, and `npm run generate:contracts:check` pass. No snapshot changed.\n- On small public projects without installed `node_modules`, the dead-code JSON output does not change. On a private monorepo with installed `node_modules`, the dependency findings do not change.","tree":{"sha":"91993aa9c4bb4ce3918fe741d70f611428bb6cdf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/git/trees/91993aa9c4bb4ce3918fe741d70f611428bb6cdf"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/git/commits/253eb87cea6d2401da30c0562c64ab59e587f6e8","verification":{"payload":"tree 91993aa9c4bb4ce3918fe741d70f611428bb6cdf\nparent a22edacb8743f67f471b175dc8657ad504591900\nauthor Bart Waardenburg <bart@waardenburg.dev> 1791249551 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791249551 +0200\n\nfix(deps): expand optional peers only when the project lists them (#3231)\n\n## Summary\n\nAn optional peer that the project does not list no longer gives credit to its own peers. Before, a listed package could escape the unused-dependency check when only such an unlisted optional peer declared it.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe peer closure in `crates/core/src/analyze/unused_deps.rs` queued every peer it found for further expansion. Since optional peers count as peers, an installed optional peer that the project does not list became a host too. Example:\n\n- The root lists `host` and `leaf` and imports only `host`.\n- `host` declares the optional peer `mid`. `mid` is installed but not listed.\n- `mid` declares `leaf` as a peer (optional or required).\n\nNothing turns on `mid`, so nothing loads `leaf`. But fallow credited `leaf` through `mid`, and `--trace-dependency leaf` said that a used package lists it.\n\n## Fix\n\n- `PeerDependencyResolver` now records for each installed peer whether `peerDependenciesMeta` marks it as optional (`PackageJson::peer_dependency_is_optional`).\n- `peer_dependency_closure` takes the names that the project lists. A required peer is expanded as before. An optional peer is expanded only when the project lists it.\n- The project has one shared install. The root `package.json` or any workspace `package.json` that lists an optional peer turns it on for that install. Thus the root check and every workspace check pass the same set: the root manifest names together with the names of every workspace manifest (`closure_listed_names`).\n- A manifest lists a name when it declares it in any dependency field, peers included. A package that declares the optional peer of a used package as its own peer expects the consumer to install it.\n- The report skips a workspace `package.json` that `ignorePatterns` matches. `peer_dependency_hosts` (used by `--trace-dependency`) now takes the configured ignore patterns, applies the same `package.json` ignore check, and builds its set with the same `closure_listed_names` function. Thus the trace and the report use the same listed set. `fallow_engine::trace::trace_dependency` takes the ignore patterns as a new argument.\n- A listed optional peer of a used package still gets credit, and still passes credit on to its own peers.\n\n## Verification\n\n- New unit test `unlisted_optional_peer_does_not_credit_its_own_peers` and new integration test `an_unlisted_optional_peer_does_not_credit_its_own_peers` with the fixture `tests/fixtures/peer-chain-through-unlisted-peer`. Both failed before the fix (`left: []`, `right: [\"leaf-a\", \"leaf-b\"]`) and pass after it. The integration test also checks the trace output for credited and uncredited leaves.\n- New unit test `optional_peer_listed_by_workspace_credits_root_peer` and new integration test `an_optional_peer_listed_by_a_workspace_credits_a_root_peer` with the fixture `tests/fixtures/peer-chain-through-workspace-peer`. The root lists `leaf`. The workspace lists `host` and its optional peer `mid` and imports `host`. `mid` declares `leaf` as a required peer. Both tests fail when the root closure uses only the root names, and pass with the fix. The integration test also checks that `--trace-dependency leaf` names `mid`.\n- New integration test `an_optional_peer_listed_by_the_root_credits_a_workspace_peer` with the fixture `tests/fixtures/peer-chain-through-root-peer`. The root lists `mid` in `devDependencies`. The workspace `app` lists `host` and `leaf` and imports `host`. `host` declares `mid` as an optional peer, and `mid` declares `leaf` as a required peer. The test failed when the workspace closure used only the workspace names (`app` reported `leaf` as unused) and passes with the fix. It also checks that `--trace-dependency leaf` names `mid`, so the trace and the report agree.\n- New integration test `an_ignored_workspace_does_not_turn_on_an_optional_peer` with the fixture `tests/fixtures/peer-chain-through-ignored-workspace`. The root lists `host` and `leaf` and imports `host`. Only `packages/ignored/package.json` lists the optional peer `mid`, and `ignorePatterns` hides that workspace. The report lists `leaf` as unused. Before this change, `--trace-dependency leaf` said `is_used: true` with `peer_of: [\"mid\"]`. After it, the trace says `is_used: false` with no `peer_of`.\n- New integration test `a_required_peer_credits_its_own_optional_peer` with the fixture `tests/fixtures/peer-chain-through-required-peer`. `host` has the required peer `mid`, and `mid` has the optional peer `leaf`, which the root lists. The report and the trace credit `leaf` through `mid`. This test passed before the change and guards the required-peer path.\n- `cargo fmt --all`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo test -p fallow-core -p fallow-engine -p fallow-api -p fallow-mcp`, the `fallow-cli` integration tests for dependencies and traces, and `npm run generate:contracts:check` pass. No snapshot changed.\n- On small public projects without installed `node_modules`, the dead-code JSON output does not change. On a private monorepo with installed `node_modules`, the dependency findings do not change.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxEyPCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAn4IQADrd13CQrO4fSkFyQnwXp2+r\nZMUF83YWgEm1ssZg545FP1QLoyPfxzw4YH6NRj8TxbBVEByIlksBsu9qwENS6lb9\nAT8Vk38XyZAlb6zX6+zRdZeBKP24TxuhtA5jVJBERsOSx3OISNhT+Nf39Vx1xupR\ngPodczUnVLPb18w/KqR+GIUCed8SyPC+u5qIRyVYdkv4COqVCXWeUaMu2aO0ekFR\nNt/5h15FK8DFrv8OBdjSq4NHV42LvS+MgraECifnVUBeDgIyFZDJRayhxSn7f8rg\nPU1g4xe/mp9pa/A9creGa9PlQw3VMttbCb41ErFDDy2k/N9gvZM3rFLbXmz8dJ36\nrJTI+yLF6UkxIYZzzIiJaP1l+qbK8Pq5fliLh7O6NW4dxUYwe72d/qcqbdRsLhVd\njmFjuzQ9Me3n4pK/8zLY+AEu6AEvmQfIKN7fMqgjAJ5EK7Z0cz91BRjw3PBXTlqv\nypvj+JRYbEu4N8LgOc+rJ9GnBy70mr+TFq6j53vuu/sZ9HABvnAFg6cCuKgon3Xi\nj+ILkDfGFPCHyWzpdjStu4PU0Lx1D1FYKIyPkBeq+jYdR82/N1f/EQ976Gcrnl3O\nBACvIb6ubGZo3gZUn0Cq8cLUFxrHISw/b2A0py2mrmthbF2emNt1PWAZFkQCt/yn\notYSsCVPOsmLaaCzKh9N\n=Orub\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-06T01:19:12Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/fallow-rs/fallow/commit/253eb87cea6d2401da30c0562c64ab59e587f6e8","node_id":"C_kwDORpPJVNoAKDI1M2ViODdjZWE2ZDI0MDFkYTMwYzA1NjJjNjRhYjU5ZTU4N2Y2ZTg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/fallow-rs/fallow/commit/a22edacb8743f67f471b175dc8657ad504591900","sha":"a22edacb8743f67f471b175dc8657ad504591900","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/a22edacb8743f67f471b175dc8657ad504591900"}],"sha":"253eb87cea6d2401da30c0562c64ab59e587f6e8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/253eb87cea6d2401da30c0562c64ab59e587f6e8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1352352?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/BartWaardenburg","id":1352352,"login":"BartWaardenburg","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNTIzNTI=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/a22edacb8743f67f471b175dc8657ad504591900/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T01:18:57Z","email":"bart@waardenburg.dev","name":"Bart Waardenburg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T01:18:57Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(deps): read the esbuild packages external signal only from build files (#3230)\n\n## Summary\n\nA consumer that depends on a private sibling gets credit for the packages that the sibling ships, because its build inlines the sibling. An explicit externalization signal removes this credit. Before this change, any file of the consumer could give the esbuild signal, test files included. Now only a build file gives the signal: a program file that a package script runs with a node runner outside test mode and that does not look like a test, or a module that such a file imports.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`collect_externalizing_workspaces` read every workspace file that imports `esbuild`. When the file contained `packages: ''external''`, the owning workspace lost the private-sibling credit. A test that calls `build({ ..., packages: \"external\", write: false })` does not change how the workspace is built. For example, a consumer builds with `vite build`, and that build inlines the sibling. A test of an esbuild plugin still removed the credit, so `fallow dead-code` reported the sibling''s package as an unused dependency of the consumer.\n\n## Fix\n\nEach workspace manifest now records the program files that its package scripts run with a node runner (`node`, `tsx`, `ts-node`, `babel-node` or `bun`), for example `build.mjs` in `node build.mjs`. The program file is the first positional argument of the runner. A runner in test mode (`node --test`, `tsx --test`, `bun test`) gives no file, and other commands give no file. So `vitest run test/plugin.test.ts` and `node --test test/plugin.test.mjs` do not make the test file a build file.\n\nA `node:test` file often runs without `--test`. So a program file that looks like a test gives no file either: `.test.` or `.spec.` in the file name, or a `test`, `tests` or `__tests__` directory in the path. Examples are `node --import tsx test/plugin.test.ts`, `tsx test/plugin.test.ts` and `node test/run.mjs`. The runner skips the value of `--tsconfig`, as it does for `--import` and `--require`.\n\nA file argument without an extension resolves the way a runtime resolves it: the exact file, then the argument with a source extension, then the index file of the directory. So `node scripts/bundle` names `scripts/bundle.mjs` or `scripts/bundle/index.mjs`. The argument must have a path separator or a `./` prefix: `node ./build` names `build.mjs`, and a bare `node build` names no file.\n\nThe build files of a workspace are these program files plus their import closure within the same workspace. The closure follows static and dynamic imports, but not type-only imports. The program file paths resolve only for a workspace that has a file that imports esbuild. So a `build.mjs` that only does `import \"./lib/bundle.mjs\"` makes `lib/bundle.mjs` a build file. The esbuild file signal applies only to a build file. The script signal (`bun build --packages=external`, `esbuild --packages=external`) does not change. Without a signal, the credit stays, which is the previous default.\n\n## Verification\n\n- New fixture `private-workspace-esbuild-external-in-test`: the consumer builds with `vite build` and has a vitest file that calls esbuild with `packages: \"external\"`. The new integration test `esbuild_external_in_test_file_keeps_bundled_credit` failed before the fix (`lodash-es` reported) and passes after it.\n- The existing `externalizing_consumer_gets_no_bundled_credit` test still passes, because its `build.mjs` runs from the `build` script.\n- Two new fixtures cover a script argument without an extension: `private-workspace-externalized-esbuild-no-extension` (`node scripts/build` runs `scripts/build.mjs`) and `private-workspace-externalized-esbuild-dir-index` (`node ./scripts/bundle` runs `scripts/bundle/index.mjs`). `externalizing_consumer_gets_no_bundled_credit` now checks both. Without the extension probe, the consumer did not report `lodash-es` for these shapes.\n- Two new fixtures name the test file in the test script: `private-workspace-esbuild-external-in-named-test` (`vitest run test/plugin.test.ts`) and `private-workspace-esbuild-external-in-node-test` (`node --test test/plugin.test.mjs` with `node:test`). `esbuild_external_in_test_file_keeps_bundled_credit` now checks both. Before the node runner rule, both reported `lodash-es`.\n- New fixture `private-workspace-externalized-esbuild-helper`: `node build.mjs` runs `build.mjs`, which imports `lib/bundle.mjs`, and `lib/bundle.mjs` calls esbuild with `packages: \"external\"`. `externalizing_consumer_gets_no_bundled_credit` now checks it. Before the import closure, the consumer did not report `lodash-es`.\n- Two new fixtures run a `node:test` file directly: `private-workspace-esbuild-external-in-tsx-import-test` (`node --import tsx test/plugin.test.ts`) and `private-workspace-esbuild-external-in-tsx-test` (`tsx test/plugin.test.ts`). `esbuild_external_in_test_file_keeps_bundled_credit` now checks both. Before the test file rule, the first one reported `lodash-es`. The unit tests also cover `node test/run.mjs`, `tests/`, `__tests__/`, `.spec.`, `--tsconfig`, `node ./build` and `node build`.\n- New unit tests `script_program_files_reads_node_runner_programs` and `script_program_files_ignores_tests_and_other_commands` cover the program file rule.\n- `cargo fmt --all`, `cargo clippy -p fallow-core --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo test -p fallow-core` and `cargo test -p fallow-cli` pass.","tree":{"sha":"08ec4f39d535b96d2265d261f9820e646f44822e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/git/trees/08ec4f39d535b96d2265d261f9820e646f44822e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/git/commits/a22edacb8743f67f471b175dc8657ad504591900","verification":{"payload":"tree 08ec4f39d535b96d2265d261f9820e646f44822e\nparent b2ffef05b942798dc82da83242bce3d90efac27e\nauthor Bart Waardenburg <bart@waardenburg.dev> 1791249537 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791249537 +0200\n\nfix(deps): read the esbuild packages external signal only from build files (#3230)\n\n## Summary\n\nA consumer that depends on a private sibling gets credit for the packages that the sibling ships, because its build inlines the sibling. An explicit externalization signal removes this credit. Before this change, any file of the consumer could give the esbuild signal, test files included. Now only a build file gives the signal: a program file that a package script runs with a node runner outside test mode and that does not look like a test, or a module that such a file imports.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`collect_externalizing_workspaces` read every workspace file that imports `esbuild`. When the file contained `packages: ''external''`, the owning workspace lost the private-sibling credit. A test that calls `build({ ..., packages: \"external\", write: false })` does not change how the workspace is built. For example, a consumer builds with `vite build`, and that build inlines the sibling. A test of an esbuild plugin still removed the credit, so `fallow dead-code` reported the sibling''s package as an unused dependency of the consumer.\n\n## Fix\n\nEach workspace manifest now records the program files that its package scripts run with a node runner (`node`, `tsx`, `ts-node`, `babel-node` or `bun`), for example `build.mjs` in `node build.mjs`. The program file is the first positional argument of the runner. A runner in test mode (`node --test`, `tsx --test`, `bun test`) gives no file, and other commands give no file. So `vitest run test/plugin.test.ts` and `node --test test/plugin.test.mjs` do not make the test file a build file.\n\nA `node:test` file often runs without `--test`. So a program file that looks like a test gives no file either: `.test.` or `.spec.` in the file name, or a `test`, `tests` or `__tests__` directory in the path. Examples are `node --import tsx test/plugin.test.ts`, `tsx test/plugin.test.ts` and `node test/run.mjs`. The runner skips the value of `--tsconfig`, as it does for `--import` and `--require`.\n\nA file argument without an extension resolves the way a runtime resolves it: the exact file, then the argument with a source extension, then the index file of the directory. So `node scripts/bundle` names `scripts/bundle.mjs` or `scripts/bundle/index.mjs`. The argument must have a path separator or a `./` prefix: `node ./build` names `build.mjs`, and a bare `node build` names no file.\n\nThe build files of a workspace are these program files plus their import closure within the same workspace. The closure follows static and dynamic imports, but not type-only imports. The program file paths resolve only for a workspace that has a file that imports esbuild. So a `build.mjs` that only does `import \"./lib/bundle.mjs\"` makes `lib/bundle.mjs` a build file. The esbuild file signal applies only to a build file. The script signal (`bun build --packages=external`, `esbuild --packages=external`) does not change. Without a signal, the credit stays, which is the previous default.\n\n## Verification\n\n- New fixture `private-workspace-esbuild-external-in-test`: the consumer builds with `vite build` and has a vitest file that calls esbuild with `packages: \"external\"`. The new integration test `esbuild_external_in_test_file_keeps_bundled_credit` failed before the fix (`lodash-es` reported) and passes after it.\n- The existing `externalizing_consumer_gets_no_bundled_credit` test still passes, because its `build.mjs` runs from the `build` script.\n- Two new fixtures cover a script argument without an extension: `private-workspace-externalized-esbuild-no-extension` (`node scripts/build` runs `scripts/build.mjs`) and `private-workspace-externalized-esbuild-dir-index` (`node ./scripts/bundle` runs `scripts/bundle/index.mjs`). `externalizing_consumer_gets_no_bundled_credit` now checks both. Without the extension probe, the consumer did not report `lodash-es` for these shapes.\n- Two new fixtures name the test file in the test script: `private-workspace-esbuild-external-in-named-test` (`vitest run test/plugin.test.ts`) and `private-workspace-esbuild-external-in-node-test` (`node --test test/plugin.test.mjs` with `node:test`). `esbuild_external_in_test_file_keeps_bundled_credit` now checks both. Before the node runner rule, both reported `lodash-es`.\n- New fixture `private-workspace-externalized-esbuild-helper`: `node build.mjs` runs `build.mjs`, which imports `lib/bundle.mjs`, and `lib/bundle.mjs` calls esbuild with `packages: \"external\"`. `externalizing_consumer_gets_no_bundled_credit` now checks it. Before the import closure, the consumer did not report `lodash-es`.\n- Two new fixtures run a `node:test` file directly: `private-workspace-esbuild-external-in-tsx-import-test` (`node --import tsx test/plugin.test.ts`) and `private-workspace-esbuild-external-in-tsx-test` (`tsx test/plugin.test.ts`). `esbuild_external_in_test_file_keeps_bundled_credit` now checks both. Before the test file rule, the first one reported `lodash-es`. The unit tests also cover `node test/run.mjs`, `tests/`, `__tests__/`, `.spec.`, `--tsconfig`, `node ./build` and `node build`.\n- New unit tests `script_program_files_reads_node_runner_programs` and `script_program_files_ignores_tests_and_other_commands` cover the program file rule.\n- `cargo fmt --all`, `cargo clippy -p fallow-core --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo test -p fallow-core` and `cargo test -p fallow-cli` pass.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxEyBCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAVusQABTLQl/0tbRNKFVd7HBC+3nk\nTJGVvcZDuCjf0UkJv+yZ/dhvaCFKoB4pCmGywbHi5rGdvCFNMnzaPF1LPyFYIECO\nOhYtwKCPTC68w8aI6khlCYvK0TW5983Ti3OtaR1OJGjLEYhTT/OmDtaeBtPfZ01J\nuYE0YRb0wS3qAbwpL50mrzyHxG3lIevp1AVLMxgQ+/o8FnEvf0FRDreotd8uOdYK\nN/6BFJ+TYT2y1oUAvMOZBx+jRgCg36X3hj9jgK/xDpeaToyKLfQgZczhsqi+wsco\n30vRxB3cEmaqBbQ9alAHuo6SpZ9jzDvW+uucOiBSLAepjsXnuWgmbJMKGbpZWIyb\nNHqR6ySIWpyuPLlYM/ww2edo+sAimqkkFMGvIpUKDy510csWJCCvH6Mk2muKuzh1\nogrOFynjvGwc0mayKFdxZi9eQTTEUMdiP8/Tv9+1GP2P/6VVKXuiaPzxX63KChPG\nx21yN+M/Q52jlZ/KmzTRCSd2nSs0KC56E8aZuEOUY3CyLfavYtjdAdQxQg+bCow2\nGq12nemqjUokkkjfahnChJ5uhzpb1pgjXoTfs1I7xsiJRvmLK9HjJ1LAYckEdAmS\nUSiomTnjOxMr/SPq2c8dEvMkuBTxRR1m/O6rnijk6Pn2mR7BQXS6AxyYVKN1e9Ir\nZVtQHG2SUbOym8puqPjN\n=qZ1g\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-06T01:18:58Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/fallow-rs/fallow/commit/a22edacb8743f67f471b175dc8657ad504591900","node_id":"C_kwDORpPJVNoAKGEyMmVkYWNiODc0M2Y2N2Y0NzFiMTc1ZGM4NjU3YWQ1MDQ1OTE5MDA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/fallow-rs/fallow/commit/b2ffef05b942798dc82da83242bce3d90efac27e","sha":"b2ffef05b942798dc82da83242bce3d90efac27e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/b2ffef05b942798dc82da83242bce3d90efac27e"}],"sha":"a22edacb8743f67f471b175dc8657ad504591900","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/a22edacb8743f67f471b175dc8657ad504591900"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1352352?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/BartWaardenburg","id":1352352,"login":"BartWaardenburg","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNTIzNTI=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/BartWaardenburg","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/commits/b2ffef05b942798dc82da83242bce3d90efac27e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T01:18:43Z","email":"bart@waardenburg.dev","name":"Bart Waardenburg"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T01:18:43Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(deps): give a root dependency one finding when no file in its scope imports it (#3229)\n\n## Summary\n\nA root `dependencies` entry could get two findings with conflicting actions. One finding was `unused-dependency` with `remove-dependency`. The other was `test-only-dependency` (default mode) or `type-only-dependency` (`--production`) with `move-to-dev`. Now such an entry gets only the `unused-dependency` finding.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe unused check attributes each import to the nearest manifest that installs the package. A root declaration is used only when an importer is outside every workspace, or when no workspace in its chain installs the package. The test-only and type-only checks did not use this attribution. They read every importer in the project.\n\nExample shape:\n\n- The root `package.json` declares `assert-lib` and `schema-lib` in `dependencies`.\n- Workspace `app` declares `schema-lib` in `dependencies` and `assert-lib` in `devDependencies`.\n- `app` imports `assert-lib` only in a test file, and `schema-lib` only with `import type`.\n\nThe unused check reported both root entries as unused. The test-only check also reported the root `assert-lib` entry, and in `--production` the type-only check also reported the root `schema-lib` entry.\n\n## Fix\n\nA new `RootImporters` helper in `crates/core/src/analyze/unused_deps.rs` returns the importers of a package that use the root declaration. It uses the same nearest-manifest rule as the unused check. `find_test_only_dependencies` and `find_type_only_dependencies` now read only these importers. A root entry without such an importer is not test-only or type-only.\n\nA project without workspaces has no change, because every importer uses the root declaration. In a monorepo, a root entry whose root importers are all test files is now test-only, also when a workspace with its own declaration imports the package at runtime.\n\n## Verification\n\n- New fixture `tests/fixtures/root-dependency-test-or-type-only-through-workspace` with the integration test `root_dependency_used_only_through_a_workspace_gets_one_finding`. The test fails before the fix (the root `assert-lib` entry also got a test-only finding) and passes after it.\n- `cargo fmt --all`, `cargo clippy -p fallow-core --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo test -p fallow-core` and `cargo test -p fallow-cli` pass. No snapshot changed.\n- The `dead-code --format json` output on several public monorepos, in default and `--production` mode, is the same before and after the fix.","tree":{"sha":"3d1751f3f08d82e0092cb505d1a8e71994b9fb6c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/git/trees/3d1751f3f08d82e0092cb505d1a8e71994b9fb6c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/fallow-rs/fallow/git/commits/b2ffef05b942798dc82da83242bce3d90efac27e","verification":{"payload":"tree 3d1751f3f08d82e0092cb505d1a8e71994b9fb6c\nparent 8fffc739dfd4ddfa5adaf704d50ae3204c0bd58f\nauthor Bart Waardenburg <bart@waardenburg.dev> 1791249523 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791249523 +0200\n\nfix(deps): give a root dependency one finding when no file in its scope imports it (#3229)\n\n## Summary\n\nA root `dependencies` entry could get two findings with conflicting actions. One finding was `unused-dependency` with `remove-dependency`. The other was `test-only-dependency` (default mode) or `type-only-dependency` (`--production`) with `move-to-dev`. Now such an entry gets only the `unused-dependency` finding.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe unused check attributes each import to the nearest manifest that installs the package. A root declaration is used only when an importer is outside every workspace, or when no workspace in its chain installs the package. The test-only and type-only checks did not use this attribution. They read every importer in the project.\n\nExample shape:\n\n- The root `package.json` declares `assert-lib` and `schema-lib` in `dependencies`.\n- Workspace `app` declares `schema-lib` in `dependencies` and `assert-lib` in `devDependencies`.\n- `app` imports `assert-lib` only in a test file, and `schema-lib` only with `import type`.\n\nThe unused check reported both root entries as unused. The test-only check also reported the root `assert-lib` entry, and in `--production` the type-only check also reported the root `schema-lib` entry.\n\n## Fix\n\nA new `RootImporters` helper in `crates/core/src/analyze/unused_deps.rs` returns the importers of a package that use the root declaration. It uses the same nearest-manifest rule as the unused check. `find_test_only_dependencies` and `find_type_only_dependencies` now read only these importers. A root entry without such an importer is not test-only or type-only.\n\nA project without workspaces has no change, because every importer uses the root declaration. In a monorepo, a root entry whose root importers are all test files is now test-only, also when a workspace with its own declaration imports the package at runtime.\n\n## Verification\n\n- New fixture `tests/fixtures/root-dependency-test-or-type-only-through-workspace` with the integration test `root_dependency_used_only_through_a_workspace_gets_one_finding`. The test fails before the fix (the root `assert-lib` entry also got a test-only finding) and passes after it.\n- `cargo fmt --all`, `cargo clippy -p fallow-core --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo test -p fallow-core` and `cargo test -p fallow-cli` pass. No snapshot changed.\n- The `dead-code --format json` output on several public monorepos, in default and `--production` mode, is the same before and after the fix.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxExzCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAjmkQAEQ4b1Qcq16rEHLcqOFH/OpP\n3V5XtwCaBk8Xl6chqZ9FNkh6jcEsvfxBVV7RtdJCm2ZkBq9OZime6I+VptV6ndmW\nOCGvwfzdFF1rhOWQ7C4Qe4c39qGk9F7vxEzQ+X4UCHVjihlwjwOt731/TOqr2TPO\nsdNItXcWA8plifdIwMmMgxG6h5XXhsN0QHVp6AfEHOq2k9m8pG/jLv3u4yFbMaWb\nxnb82YaGQJphdTc6QKAzrg1O6626LMZlWIySZrFf4GD/z6xmqdtwEXgSTFteUvIF\nVNHzgiCHUSpPaWD6H